course: dictionary
lesson: 9103
title: The -ish ending
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: The ending -ish makes a word less exact. Smallish means fairly small: a bit small, but not very small. Tallish, greenish and sevenish work the same way.
explain: In «The goliath and Hercules beetles are among the biggest insects; insects stay smallish partly because their breathing tubes work well only over short distances.» the word smallish tells you insects are not very big, but it does not give an exact size.
explain: Ask yourself: is the writer giving an exact amount, or a rough feeling? Smallish is a rough feeling. In a maths problem, smallish is not a number, so it cannot give you an answer.
fact: The ending -ish means somewhat or fairly, as in smallish, tallish and reddish.
fact: «Smallish» means fairly small: a bit small, but not very small.
fact: Adding -ish softens a description, so it is less exact.
fact: «Sevenish» means around seven, not exactly seven.
fact: Smallish is not a number, so you cannot use it to solve a maths problem. You need an exact value.
exercise: The beetle is smallish. What does smallish mean? | Invisible | Huge and heavy | *Fairly small, but not very small | Exactly two centimetres
exercise: Which word means around seven, not exactly seven? | *Sevenish | Seven | Sevenfold | Seventeen
exercise: «Insects stay smallish partly because their breathing tubes work well only over short distances.» Which word shows the size is rough, not exact? | breathing | insects | *smallish | distances
test: Maria says the box is tallish. Is that an exact measurement? | Yes, it is 100 centimetres | Yes, it is exactly one metre | No, it is taller than a giraffe | *No, it is a rough description
test: Alex needs the exact answer to 3 plus 4. Can he use sevenish? | Yes, because it means seven | No, because it is too long | *No, a maths answer needs an exact number | Yes, sevenish is always right
exam: Which sentence uses smallish correctly? | The answer is smallish: 12 | The clock struck smallish at noon | Smallish is the number after five | *The garden was smallish, but big enough for a swing
